# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 3 ways: aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3 has "gradation ∧ more_less_by_resemblance → maximum_exists" and "more_less_by_resemblance" and "maximum_exists ∧ greatest_truth_greatest_being → uttermost_being" and "greatest_truth_greatest_being"; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 has "more_less_by_resemblance → (gradation → maximum_exists)" and "greatest_truth_greatest_being" and "more_less_by_resemblance"; they differ when only "among beings there are some more and some less good, true, noble and the like", "more and less are predicated of things according as they resemble something which is the maximum", "there is something which is truest, something best, something noblest" and "those things that are greatest in truth are greatest in being" are true, which the second allows; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3 has "more_less_by_resemblance" and "greatest_truth_greatest_being"
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3, to: Suppose "among beings there are some more and some less good, true, noble and the like", "more and less are predicated of things according as they resemble something which is the maximum", "there is something which is truest, something best, something noblest" and "those things that are greatest in truth are greatest in being" are true and "there is something which is uttermost being" is false. Is S3, "But more and less are predicated of different things, according as they resemble in their different ways something which is the maximum, as a thing is said to be hotter according as it more nearly resembles that which is hottest; so that there is something which is truest, something best, something noblest and, consequently, something which is uttermost being; for those things that are greatest in truth are greatest in being, as it is written in Metaph. ii.", true then?
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3 has "maximum_causes_genus" and "uttermost_being ∧ maximum_causes_genus → cause_of_all_being"; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3 have "maximum_causes_genus"; they differ when only "there is something which is uttermost being" and "the maximum in any genus is the cause of all in that genus" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is something which is uttermost being" and "the maximum in any genus is the cause of all in that genus" are true and "there is something which is to all beings the cause of their being, goodness, and every other perfection" is false. Is S4, "Now the maximum in any genus is the cause of all in that genus; as fire, which is the maximum heat, is the cause of all hot things.", true then?
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3 has nothing from it; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3 have "cause_of_all_being → god_exists"; they differ when only "there is something which is to all beings the cause of their being, goodness, and every other perfection" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is something which is to all beings the cause of their being, goodness, and every other perfection" is true and "there is something which we call God" is false. Is S5, "Therefore there must also be something which is to all beings the cause of their being, goodness, and every other perfection; and this we call God.", true then?
aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3 has "cause_of_all_being → god_exists"; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 has "maximum_exists ∧ greatest_truth_greatest_being → uttermost_being" and "uttermost_being ∧ maximum_causes_genus → cause_of_all_being"; they differ when only "there is something which is truest, something best, something noblest", "those things that are greatest in truth are greatest in being", "there is something which is uttermost being" and "the maximum in any genus is the cause of all in that genus" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3 has "gradation ∧ more_less_by_resemblance → maximum_exists" and "maximum_exists ∧ greatest_truth_greatest_being → uttermost_being" and "uttermost_being ∧ maximum_causes_genus → cause_of_all_being"
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.3 have "god_exists"; aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2 has "cause_of_all_being ∧ god_exists"; they differ when only "there is something which we call God" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3.2, to: Suppose "there is something which we call God" is true and "there is something which is to all beings the cause of their being, goodness, and every other perfection" is false. Is the conclusion, "Therefore there must also be something which is to all beings the cause of their being, goodness, and every other perfection; and this we call God.", true then?
aquinas-fourth-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-fourth-way.logic.r3 P8 (S4): the sentence has no word of "there is something which is uttermost being": is it the sentence P8 came from?
